.SH SESSION PATHS
Session paths use a concrete signed node ID and a path relative to that node's
explicitly exported root:
.PP
.nf
NODE:/relative/path
node:NODE:/relative/path
.fi
.PP
The form
.B user@host:/path
remains SSH/SCP syntax and is not rewritten. The form
.B NODE:~/path
is undefined because session paths are rooted in explicit exports.
.SH BASIC SESSION EXAMPLE
On the workstation:
.PP
.nf
wv session-host \
  --root "$HOME/shared" \
  --tcp-allow '127.0.0.1:8080,*.corp.internal:443' \
  --node-context workstation.context.json \
  --public-key-file issuer.key.pub \
  -- ssh -X user@compute-node
.fi
.PP
Inside the resulting remote shell:
.PP
.nf
wv attach \
  --node-context compute-node.context.json \
  --public-key-file issuer.key.pub \
  --root "$HOME/compute-shared" \
  --tcp-allow '127.0.0.1:3000,db.internal:5432' \
  --udp-allow '127.0.0.1:53,*.internal:53'
.fi
.PP
Keep
.B wv attach
active. From another shell owned by the same remote account:
.PP
.nf
wv api --json topology
wv cp ./input.dat compute-node:/jobs/input.dat
wv cp compute-node:/results/result.bin ./result.bin
wv connect compute-node 127.0.0.1:3000
.fi
.SH RECURSIVE SESSIONS
Recursive receiving SSH servers must accept both variables:
.PP
.nf
AcceptEnv WVORIGIN WVHOP
.fi
.PP
Use
.B session-host --recursive
with a dedicated hop-signing identity and an OpenSSH allowed-signers file. Each
hop sets
.B WVORIGIN
to the immediate previous node and carries the complete signed ancestry in
.B WVHOP .
.PP
Agent forwarding is security-sensitive. Prefer dedicated hop keys, narrow signer
policies, short TTLs, and node-local agents on trusted intermediates.
.SH SOCKS5 PROOF EXAMPLE
Validate a proof policy:
.PP
.nf
wv socks-policy validate socks-policy.json
.fi
.PP
Require signed TCP requests on the final node by passing
.B --tcp-proof-policy
and
.B --tcp-require-proof
to
.B wv attach .
Start a loopback proxy:
.PP
.nf
wv session-proxy \
  --node compute-node \
  --auth proof \
  --proof-policy socks-policy.json \
  --listen 127.0.0.1:1080
.fi
.PP
Then connect with a pinned server ID, canonical policy digest, final node,
principal, and signing identity using
.B wv socks-connect .
.SH SECURITY
Node registration is signed and bound to the upgraded session. Services require
advertised signed capabilities plus installed local implementations. TCP and UDP
destination policies are independent and deny by default.
.PP
Proof-aware SOCKS authorization does not make a fully compromised final host
trustworthy. A privileged attacker on that host can replace binaries or policy,
inspect memory, bypass the service, or dial directly. Stronger assurance requires
measured boot, remote attestation, a trusted execution environment, or an
authorization service outside that administrator's control.
.PP
Proof authentication does not encrypt a non-loopback client-to-proxy TCP hop.
Keep listeners loopback-only or protect that hop separately.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B WVORIGIN
Concrete signed ID of the immediate previous node for the current SSH hop.
.TP
.B WVHOP
Complete SSHSIG-authenticated recursive ancestry, when recursive mode is used.
.TP
.B DISPLAY
OpenSSH-provided X11 display used by the dynamic-session bootstrap.
.TP
.B SSH_AUTH_SOCK
OpenSSH agent socket used when a command selects an SSH-agent signer.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Commands return zero on success and non-zero on validation, authorization,
transport, policy, or usage failure. Usage errors conventionally return 2.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/weaverssh/
Typical location for node contexts, public verification keys, signer policy,
and example configuration.
.TP
.I ~/.ssh/
Typical location for dedicated SSHSIG hop keys and SOCKS principal keys.
.TP
.I man/wv.1
Source manpage in the repository.
